for every forgettable blockbuster that  has come and gone there's been a steady  stream of stellar under-the-radar action  flicks what they lack in terms of scale  and big-budget effects they make up for  with their share of bone shattering  brawls white-knuckle chases and bloody  bombast a few years back writer director  Ted Gaugin made a name for himself among  the genre set with the atmospheric  haunted house film we are still here  though the films no budget production  limited what Gaugin could ultimately  accomplish there was enough style on  display to get excited about what the  filmmaker would do next  surprisingly instead of horror Gaugin  jumps to gory action in mohawk Mohawk  unfolds in the last days of the war of  1812 and finds a young Mohawk woman and  her lover's squaring off against a  violent band of American soldiers Mohawk  might flag a bit in dialogue heavy  scenes but when Gaugin leaves words out  of the mix this under scene actioner has  as much energy and vision as any  blockbuster if you've got an Amazon  Prime subscription then you're probably  aware of the streaming services new  action series Hanna what you might not  be aware of is that the show is based on  a 2011 film of the same name that film  ended up doing moderately well at the  global box office but it feels like it's  been all but forgotten by audiences in  the years since like the series the film  version of Hanna follows a teenage girl  who has a penchant for putting down bad  guys with a graceful brutality all the  while she's hunted by a secretive  intelligence operative a mirthfully  menacing cate blanchett hannah is a taut  boldly stylized pseudo detective action  fantasy that delivers a weighted payload  in terms of both pulse-pounding  set-pieces and character driven drama  it's high time this action-packed gem  got rediscovered I just missed your  heart  for many American moviegoers exposure to  the work of Japanese extreme auteur  Takashi Miike begins and ends with his  dark 1999 masterwork audition  understandable as it may be you're doing  yourself a disservice by ignoring his  other films among meuk a sprawling  output you'll find the work of a  visionary filmmaker with a flair for  genre a penchant for gore and plenty of  eye popping action you get plenty of all  that in me case 2010 samurai stunner 13  assassins set in the waning days of  feudal Japan the film setup promises a  harrowing showdown between the title  assassins and the Shogun's ruthless  brother whose upcoming promotion  threatens to bring chaos to the  otherwise peaceful Japanese landscape  Mika takes his time getting to that  showdown meticulously building toward  the fateful finale with a screenplay  that allows audiences to get good and  comfy with the anti heroes and villains  of the piece when meet Kay finally blows  the lid off 13 assassins he does so with  a bloody brutal savagery all but unseen  in American cinema wheelman follows a  freshly paroled getaway driver played by  Frank Grillo who finds himself  double-crossed and on the run fittingly  for a movie following a character played  by longtime character actor Grillo the  film is very much the sort of pulpy  crime flick that plays to his strengths  writer director Jeremy rush gives his  star ample room to talk tough act up and  drive hard rush amplifies the film's  inherent machismo by keeping his camera  confined almost entirely within a single  car it's a bold stylish move that pays  off in spades giving a fresh perspective  to the sort of car crashing fist  fighting and machine gunning we've seen  a million times over Geneva  don't want to talk chitchat I don't  chitchat about the job one day the world  at large will catch up to the genius of  writer director Joe Cornish when it does  the likely look back on 2011 s attacked  the block and wonder how it ever became  a box-office bomb hornish's film  delivers the action-packed stoner comedy  alien invasion horror flick the world  never knew it needed  sadly the world still seems oblivious to  the sharply written exquisitely executed  genre mashup despite the jaw-dropping  action scenes and killer creature design  not to mention what should have been  star making turns from future Doctor Who  star Jodie Whittaker and later Star Wars  star John boyega do yourself a favor and  check out this hidden gem though the  bloodshed has finally stopped the  simmering tensions that drove Irish  Catholics and Protestants headlong into  the carnage of the troubles remains a  part of life in Northern Ireland in  Belfast circa 1971 the setting of 71 the  divisive fires that drove the madness  still burned with the raging heat of  bombed out cars and gunpowder 71 opens  with a kindly new recruit joining a  British military regiment in the  war-torn streets of Belfast the film  pivots sharply when an angry mob ends up  separating the man from his retreating  regiment leaving him lost alone and  injured in a situation where he's  perceived as an enemy to one and all  from there the film plunges viewers into  a breathless night long race for  survival through an unforgiving  landscape where vengeance is righteous  no matter the cost  71 is that rarest of the action films  that dares to examine both the human  cost of the bloody conflict and a cycle  of hate that fuelled it in case you  missed out on haywire in theatres the  film follows a tough-as-nails black ops  agent called Mallory who finds herself  hunted by international mercenaries  after being double-crossed in a  clandestine mission with plenty of grit  brawn and brains  Mallory quickly turns the tides on her  betrayers setting up a hunted becomes  the hunter revenge tale for the ages  though haywire marked director Steven  Soderbergh first full  action flick you'd never be able to tell  by how effortlessly he executes the  film's intricate set pieces style and  whimsy most action movies never even  aspire to that Soderbergh cast legit mmm  a star gina carano in the lead role only  serves to legitimize each vicious  escalation even as the film  unapologetically begins to earn its  title it all makes haywire and overlooks  diamond among Soderbergh impeccable  filmography if you'd have told us a few  years back that we'd be listing a  certified fresh' Tom Cruise fronted  sci-fi epic among our underrated action  movies picks we probably would have told  you you were completely insane yet here  we are half a decade after its release  still wondering how so many people slept  on this intelligent action-packed Bangor  edge of tomorrow which follows a soldier  continuously killed during a deadly  alien assault only to be dropped right  back into the action is worth  remembering this Groundhog Day esque  action film is a classic one that  matches epic human drama against an  equally epic scope it's also a film with  an insanely cool sci-fi concept with  mind-boggling special effects and set  pieces that make repeat viewings  endlessly rewarding if you haven't seen  it go watch it if you have seen it see  it again I would go with the a sense of  manifest destiny rags-to-riches rapid  rise through the ranks born to deliver  us and the people they love that sort of  thing few genres are is well primed to  build big-time action into a narrative  as science-fiction while Hollywood has  given us its share of super-sized sci-fi  bombast over the years audiences have  proven themselves equally open to  experiencing more concept driven  science-fiction fare while Leigh  Whannell scamp tinged techno-thriller  upgrade sits firmly in the latter  category this unpredictable tale wholly  embraces its big-budget ambition it's  the sort of savvy small-scale  science-fiction that deserved a much  broader audience if you're among those  who missed upgrade in theaters it  follows a technophobic every man who  blazes a path of bloody vengeance after  a mugging leaves him paralyzed and his  wife dead he's aided in that endeavor by  stem a computer chip that 