ase it's an oversight that  action fans will want to rectify  immediately because this blood-soaked  crime drama packs a punch you won't be  able to shake at the center of that saga  is a former triad enforcer named Ito who  while slaughtering an entire village for  his bosses stays the execution of a  young girl in doing so he sets off a  bloody chain of events that puts the  Triad on their trail matters get  insanely violent from that moment on  with a ceaseless  wave of triad henchmen armed with cool  names cool weapons and cooler than cool  attitudes taking on eco and a few loyal  members of his old crew bones are broken  limbs are severed bodies are set a flame  or blown to bits through all the  grueling meticulously orchestrated  action the film also manages to capture  a deeply human story of redemption for  action fans of a certain age the late  80s remain a fertile time period for a  particular sort of absurdly predictable  and way over-the-top action flick  there's no doubt that genre maven Adam  Wingard is one of those fans and a  stylized horror tinge thriller the guest  is all the proof you'll ever need for  the uninitiated being predictable and  over-the-top aren't really hindrances to  this sort of film in fact the mixture of  seriousness and absurdity is all part of  the fun wyngarde and screenwriters Simon  Barrett have about as much fun with that  approach in the guests as a filmmakers  have ever had leading the charge for the  film's giddily over-the-top vibe is Dan  Stevens who delivers a fascinatingly  unhinged performance as a mysterious  soldier who befriends an unsuspecting  family  he's ostensibly there to pay respects to  their son who supposedly died in combat  while serving alongside Stevens  character without spoiling any of the  dizzying madness we'll just say that the  man isn't who he claims to be as bodies  start piling up around town the guest  twists and turns from eerily stolen  identity drama into a surreal neo-noir  horror show 